text:
4.18 Try it yourself
These exercises use the table bids, and the objects tender_list, stopped, winners, firm_record and group_screens() of this chapter. Try each one before you open the solution.


Firms that bid much less. List the firms that bid at least three times in a district in 2022-23, and fewer than half as many times there in 2024-25. Then count the tenders of each such district in the two years.






TipSolution






ex_less &lt;- bids |&gt;
  count(firm, district, fy) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = fy, values_from = n, values_fill = 0) |&gt;
  filter(`2022-23` &gt;= 3, `2024-25` &lt; `2022-23` / 2)
ex_less

# A tibble: 4 × 5
  firm  district `2022-23` `2023-24` `2024-25`
  &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;int&gt;     &lt;int&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1 F06   Chandpur        15        16         7
2 F14   Chandpur        17        15         0
3 F15   Bhimganj        15         0         0
4 F20   Bhimganj         7         5         1

ex_work &lt;- tender_list |&gt;
  filter(district %in% ex_less$district, fy %in% c("2022-23", "2024-25")) |&gt;
  count(district, fy) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = fy, values_from = n)
ex_work

# A tibble: 2 × 3
  district `2022-23` `2024-25`
  &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;int&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1 Bhimganj        27        26
2 Chandpur        36        18


4 firm and district combinations appear, against 2 in the chapter. Chandpur had 36 tenders in 2022-23 but only 18 in 2024-25. So part of the fall there is simply less work. Bhimganj had 27 and 26 tenders, almost the same. Yet F20, an outsider to the group, also bid far less there. This fits the picture of a district closed to outsiders. A fall in a firm’s bids means something only when the work available did not fall too.





All the losing bids. Screen 4 used only the runner-up. For the Bhimganj group’s tenders from 2023-24 in which only group firms bid, find how far every losing bid sits above the winner. What share of these gaps lie between 2 and 5 points? Compare with all other tenders that have two or more bids.






TipSolution






ex_gaps &lt;- bids |&gt;
  filter(n_bids &gt;= 2) |&gt;
  mutate(gap = quoted_pct - min(quoted_pct),
         closed_group = all(firm %in% c("F03", "F07", "F11")) & district == "Bhimganj" &
           fy != "2022-23",
         .by = tender_id) |&gt;
  filter(rank &gt; 1) |&gt;
  summarise(losing_bids = n(), share_2_to_5 = round(mean(gap &gt;= 2 & gap &lt;= 5), 2),
            .by = closed_group)
ex_gaps

# A tibble: 2 × 3
  closed_group losing_bids share_2_to_5
  &lt;lgl&gt;              &lt;int&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;
1 FALSE                833         0.36
2 TRUE                  48         1   


In the group’s closed tenders, 100% of all losing bids lie between 2 and 5 points above the winner. In other tenders the share is 36%. Genuine losing bids spread widely. The group’s covers sit in a narrow band.





Did the old firms follow the newcomer? In Devnagar, leave out the tenders won by F22. Compare the mean ratio of the other winning bids before and after F22’s first bid.






TipSolution






ex_f22_first &lt;- min(bids$date[bids$firm == "F22"])
ex_dev &lt;- winners |&gt;
  filter(district == "Devnagar", firm != "F22") |&gt;
  mutate(period = if_else(date &lt; ex_f22_first, "1 Before", "2 After")) |&gt;
  summarise(tenders = n(), mean_ratio = round(mean(ratio), 3), .by = period)
ex_dev

# A tibble: 2 × 3
  period   tenders mean_ratio
  &lt;chr&gt;      &lt;int&gt;      &lt;dbl&gt;
1 1 Before      36      0.96 
2 2 After       24      0.897


Even without the newcomer’s own wins, the mean ratio fell from 0.960 to 0.897. The local firms themselves bid lower once the newcomer was in the market. This supports the reading that the earlier prices were held up, though it still does not prove it.





Is the pair from Eklavyapur rotating? Run the reshuffling test of Section 4.6 on the wins of F17 and F18 in the tenders where both of them bid. With two firms, a repeat has a chance of about one half.






TipSolution






ex_w &lt;- bids |&gt;
  filter(sum(firm %in% c("F17", "F18")) == 2, .by = tender_id) |&gt;
  filter(winner, firm %in% c("F17", "F18")) |&gt;
  arrange(date) |&gt;
  pull(firm)
ex_rep &lt;- sum(ex_w[-1] == ex_w[-length(ex_w)])
set.seed(2025)
ex_shuf &lt;- replicate(10000, {
  s &lt;- sample(ex_w)
  sum(s[-1] == s[-length(s)])
})
c(wins = length(ex_w), repeats = ex_rep, mean_shuffled = round(mean(ex_shuf), 1),
  p = mean(ex_shuf &lt;= ex_rep))

         wins       repeats mean_shuffled             p 
       25.000        14.000        11.700         0.876 


The pair won 25 of these tenders, with 14 repeats against 11.7 in a typical shuffle. The share of shuffles with as few repeats is 0.88, so there is no sign of turns. That is not surprising. A phantom pair is one business, and has no need to share the work between its two names. Different schemes leave different traces, which is why we use many screens.





Different weights. The audit team decides that a register link and identical BOQ rates should each count as 2, because they point directly to contact between bidders. Recompute the scores with these weights, and see whether the order of the groups changes.






TipSolution






ex_scores &lt;- map(list(c("F03", "F07", "F11"), c("F17", "F18"), c("F08", "F24"),
                      c("F21", "F23", "F25"), c("F13", "F16"), c("F02", "F12")),
                 group_screens) |&gt;
  list_rbind() |&gt;
  mutate(score = (closed_share &gt;= 0.5) + coalesce(margin_sd &lt;= 1.5, FALSE) +
           coalesce(inside_gap &gt;= 3, FALSE) + (win_ratio &gt;= 0.97) +
           2 * register_link + 2 * identical_rates) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(score)) |&gt;
  select(group, score)
ex_scores

# A tibble: 6 × 2
  group       score
  &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;
1 F03 F07 F11     4
2 F17 F18         4
3 F08 F24         2
4 F21 F23 F25     0
5 F13 F16         0
6 F02 F12         0


With the new weights, the Bhimganj group scores 4, F17 and F18 score 4, and F08 and F24 score 2. No comparison group scores more than 0. The phantom pair has caught up with the ring. The weights changed how the suspects compare with each other, which is why they must be fixed and justified before the scores are seen.





A lower bar for losers. Rerun Screen 9 on firms with at least 20 bids and a win share below 0.3, using firm_record. For each firm, find the standard deviation of its margin above the firm that beat it most often. Which firm has the steadiest margin?






TipSolution






ex_lose &lt;- firm_record |&gt;
  filter(bids &gt;= 20, win_share &lt; 0.3)

ex_losses &lt;- bids |&gt;
  filter(firm %in% ex_lose$firm, !winner) |&gt;
  left_join(bids |&gt;
              filter(winner) |&gt;
              select(tender_id, beaten_by = firm, win_pct = quoted_pct),
            by = "tender_id") |&gt;
  mutate(margin = quoted_pct - win_pct)

ex_rival &lt;- ex_losses |&gt;
  count(firm, beaten_by, sort = TRUE) |&gt;
  slice(1, .by = firm)

ex_steady &lt;- ex_losses |&gt;
  semi_join(ex_rival, by = c("firm", "beaten_by")) |&gt;
  summarise(main_rival = first(beaten_by), losses = n(),
            sd_margin = round(sd(margin), 2), .by = firm) |&gt;
  arrange(sd_margin)
ex_steady

# A tibble: 10 × 4
   firm  main_rival losses sd_margin
   &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;int&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
 1 F18   F17            14      0.56
 2 F25   F16             8      1.82
 3 F23   F16            10      1.87
 4 F02   F12            15      2.39
 5 F09   F15            11      2.67
 6 F16   F13            12      3.05
 7 F08   F02             8      3.05
 8 F01   F24             8      3.45
 9 F10   F15            12      3.52
10 F06   F15            13      4.02


10 firms now pass, against 6 in the chapter. F18 still has the steadiest margin, 0.56, against F17. Next come F25 and F23, both of Devnagar and both beaten most often by F16, at 1.82 and 1.87. Two Devnagar firms losing to the same rival by fairly steady margins fits the reading of Screen 6, that a quiet arrangement held prices up before the newcomer came. It is a lead to check against the dates, not a finding.

6.7 What earlier chapters add
Several tests from earlier chapters apply directly to payables. We run each in a few lines here. The chapters cited explain them in full.
Duplicate invoices and payments, Chapter 34 and Section 34.6. We look for the same invoice number and amount under any vendor, and for the same vendor and amount with different invoice numbers.

dup_any_vendor &lt;- invoices |&gt;
  filter(n() &gt; 1, .by = c(inv_no, inv_date, amount))
dup_any_vendor |&gt;
  select(inv_id, vendor_id, inv_no, inv_date, po_no, amount)

# A tibble: 2 × 6
  inv_id   vendor_id inv_no  inv_date   po_no         amount
  &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;date&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;
1 INV00052 V226      MDM/643 2024-05-01 PO/24-25/0020 30202.
2 INV00056 V293      MDM/643 2024-05-01 &lt;NA&gt;          30202.

ssd &lt;- invoices |&gt;
  filter(n_distinct(inv_no) &gt; 1, .by = c(vendor_id, amount))
ssd |&gt;
  select(inv_id, vendor_id, inv_no, inv_date, po_no, amount)

# A tibble: 4 × 6
  inv_id   vendor_id inv_no  inv_date   po_no         amount
  &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;date&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;
1 INV00110 V192      JAC/511 2024-05-19 PO/24-25/0111 114000
2 INV00404 V192      JAC/125 2024-08-19 &lt;NA&gt;          114000
3 INV00842 V130      MHS/615 2024-12-25 PO/24-25/0835  64965
4 INV00867 V130      MHS/200 2024-12-31 PO/24-25/0890  64965


The first test finds the same bill, MDM/643 for ₹30,202, under both Maa Durga records. The genuine vendor was paid against an order. The new record was paid again, with no order, after it was registered. The bill is dated 1 May 2024, before the second record even existed. This is the duplicate payment that a duplicate vendor makes possible. The second test finds pairs of bills of one vendor for the same amount. One pair is Jai Ambe Cement Depot, where a bill with an order and a later bill with no order are for the same 300 bags and the same amount. The bill with no order may be the same supply billed again. The other pair is two orders of the same quantity a few days apart, with orders and receipts for both, which looks genuine.

f_dup &lt;- unique(c(dup_any_vendor$vendor_id, ssd$vendor_id[is.na(ssd$po_no)]))

Bills split below a limit, Section 15.17 and Chapter 33. Suppose that a district office may approve a purchase of up to ₹2.5 lakh, and larger ones go to the Directorate. We count bills between ₹2 lakh and ₹2.5 lakh for each office, vendor, item and month.

split_check &lt;- invoices |&gt;
  filter(amount &gt;= 2e5, amount &lt; 2.5e5) |&gt;
  mutate(month = floor_date(inv_date, "month")) |&gt;
  summarise(bills = n(), total = sum(amount), first = min(inv_date), last = max(inv_date),
            .by = c(office, vendor_id, item, month)) |&gt;
  filter(bills &gt;= 3)
split_check

# A tibble: 1 × 8
  office   vendor_id item        month      bills  total first      last      
  &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;date&gt;     &lt;int&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;date&gt;     &lt;date&gt;    
1 Madhopur V221      LED bulb 9W 2024-12-01     4 969130 2024-12-15 2024-12-23


Madhopur paid 4 bills of Sagar Lighting House for LED bulbs, all between ₹2 lakh and ₹2.5 lakh, from 15 December 2024 to 23 December 2024. Together they come to ₹9.7 lakh, far above the office’s limit. This is the vendor that is paid into an employee’s account.
Vendor names spelt differently, Chapter 35 and Section 34.8. We compare every pair of vendor names with the Jaro-Winkler distance and keep the closest.

vendors |&gt;
  inner_join(vendors, by = join_by(vendor_id &lt; vendor_id), suffix = c("_1", "_2")) |&gt;
  mutate(jw = round(stringdist(str_to_lower(vendor_name_1), str_to_lower(vendor_name_2),
                               method = "jw", p = 0.1), 3)) |&gt;
  filter(jw &lt; 0.06) |&gt;
  arrange(jw) |&gt;
  select(vendor_id_1, vendor_name_1, vendor_id_2, vendor_name_2, jw)

# A tibble: 3 × 5
  vendor_id_1 vendor_name_1              vendor_id_2 vendor_name_2            jw
  &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;chr&gt;                      &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;chr&gt;                 &lt;dbl&gt;
1 V204        Kaveri Electricals         V259        Kaveri Electricals    0    
2 V226        Maa Durga Medical Agencies V293        Maa Durga Medical Ag… 0.031
3 V111        Sagar Medical Agencies     V187        Satpura Medical Agen… 0.058


The two Kaveri records and the two Maa Durga records come first, as the PAN test found. The next pair, Sagar Medical Agencies and Satpura Medical Agencies, shares the common words “Medical Agencies”, and the two have different PANs and addresses. Name similarity alone is weak. Together with the same PAN or address, it is strong.
Vendors at the same place, Section 57.7. We write addresses in one form, removing words like “Shop” and “No.” and expanding “Rd”, and look for vendors at the same address.

norm_addr &lt;- function(x) {
  x |&gt;
    str_to_lower() |&gt;
    str_replace_all("\\brd\\b", "road") |&gt;
    str_remove_all("\\b(shop|no|plot|house)\\b") |&gt;
    str_remove_all("[^a-z0-9]")
}

vendors |&gt;
  mutate(place = norm_addr(address)) |&gt;
  filter(n() &gt; 1, .by = place) |&gt;
  select(vendor_id, vendor_name, address)

# A tibble: 2 × 3
  vendor_id vendor_name                address                          
  &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;                      &lt;chr&gt;                            
1 V226      Maa Durga Medical Agencies Shop No. 7, Station Road, Sitapur
2 V293      Maa Durga Medical Agency   Shop 7, Station Rd, Sitapur      


The two Maa Durga records turn out to be in the same shop, written two ways.
The rate in force, Section 16.6. Cement is bought on a State rate contract, whose rate was lowered from 1 October 2024. A rolling join finds the rate in force on each order date.

old_rate &lt;- purchase_orders |&gt;
  filter(item == "Cement bag") |&gt;
  left_join(rate_contract, by = join_by(item, closest(po_date &gt;= effective_from))) |&gt;
  filter(rate != contract_rate)
old_rate |&gt;
  select(po_no, po_date, office, vendor_id, qty, rate, contract_rate)

# A tibble: 3 × 7
  po_no         po_date    office    vendor_id   qty  rate contract_rate
  &lt;chr&gt;         &lt;date&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt;
1 PO/24-25/0721 2024-11-06 Chandgarh V192        124   380           355
2 PO/24-25/0749 2024-11-18 Chandgarh V158        116   380           355
3 PO/24-25/0792 2024-11-28 Chandgarh V158        130   380           355


3 orders of Chandgarh were placed at the old rate after it was lowered, an overpayment of ₹9,250.
Other chapters. The payments in this chapter also appear as journal entries in the ledger. Chapter 1 tests those entries for override, such as bills just below an approval limit or entries made at night. The payments must agree with the bank, which Chapter 2 reconciles item by item. Large orders should have gone to tender, and Chapter 3 checks the tenders themselves. An order split to avoid a tender appears both here and there.

7.3 First, is the data complete?
A test on incomplete data proves nothing. Chapter 2 reconciled records item by item. Here a quicker check is enough. First, the keys of the two masters must be unique.

c(employees  = anyDuplicated(employees$emp_code),
  pensioners = anyDuplicated(pensioners$ppo_no))

 employees pensioners 
         0          0 


anyDuplicated() returns 0 when nothing is repeated. Next, every employee paid should be in the master. anti_join() keeps the rows with no partner.

payroll |&gt;
  anti_join(employees, by = "emp_code") |&gt;
  count(emp_code, office)

# A tibble: 1 × 3
  emp_code office       n
  &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;int&gt;
1 070815   Belkheda     3


One code, 070815, is paid by the Belkheda office for three months, but is not in the master. We note it, and come back to it in Section 7.8. Finally, the salaries and arrears in our data should add up to what the bank paid out, month by month.

arrears_by_month &lt;- arrears |&gt;
  mutate(month = floor_date(paid_on, "month")) |&gt;
  summarise(arrears = sum(amount), .by = month)

payroll |&gt;
  summarise(salaries = sum(net_pay), .by = month) |&gt;
  left_join(arrears_by_month, by = "month") |&gt;
  mutate(arrears = coalesce(arrears, 0)) |&gt;
  left_join(bank_salary, by = "month") |&gt;
  mutate(difference = salaries + arrears - bank_debits) |&gt;
  print(n = 12)

# A tibble: 12 × 5
   month      salaries arrears bank_debits difference
   &lt;date&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;      &lt;dbl&gt;
 1 2024-04-01 56422228       0    56422228          0
 2 2024-05-01 56512850       0    56512850          0
 3 2024-06-01 56308717       0    56308717          0
 4 2024-07-01 57666539       0    57666539          0
 5 2024-08-01 57780611       0    57780611          0
 6 2024-09-01 57688275       0    57688275          0
 7 2024-10-01 58381194   55050    58436244          0
 8 2024-11-01 58065566   15570    58081136          0
 9 2024-12-01 58218960  227028    58445988          0
10 2025-01-01 57981627   95733    58077360          0
11 2025-02-01 57793828       0    57793828          0
12 2025-03-01 57419818       0    57419818          0


coalesce() turns a missing arrears total into 0, for months with no arrears bill. Every month agrees. The year’s salaries come to ₹69.02 crore in 10,830 monthly payments, and arrears to ₹3.9 lakh. The same check works for pensions.

pension_paid |&gt;
  summarise(pension = sum(amount), .by = month) |&gt;
  left_join(commutation |&gt;
              mutate(month = floor_date(paid_on, "month")) |&gt;
              summarise(commuted = sum(amount), .by = month),
            by = "month") |&gt;
  left_join(treasury_pension, by = "month") |&gt;
  summarise(difference = sum(pension + coalesce(commuted, 0) - treasury_debits))

# A tibble: 1 × 1
  difference
       &lt;dbl&gt;
1          0


This agrees too. Pensions of ₹42.82 crore were paid to 1,572 pensioners in the year. In real work, a difference here is the first thing to settle.

7.4 Changes in the employee master
The employee master decides who is paid and how much, and its change log shows how that changed. So we start here.

7.4.1 Paid before joining or after leaving
An employee may be paid only for the days in service. So a salary for a month that ends before the date of joining, or begins after the date of leaving, is wrong. Leaving covers resignation, retirement, death and transfer.

outside &lt;- payroll |&gt;
  inner_join(employees |&gt; select(emp_code, doj, dol, leave_reason), by = "emp_code") |&gt;
  mutate(month_end = ceiling_date(month, "month") - 1) |&gt;
  filter(month_end &lt; doj | (!is.na(dol) & month &gt; dol))

outside |&gt;
  select(emp_code, office, month, days_paid, doj, dol, leave_reason, net_pay)

# A tibble: 3 × 8
  emp_code office    month      days_paid doj        dol        leave_reason
  &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;date&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;date&gt;     &lt;date&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;       
1 023305   Motinagar 2024-09-01        30 2022-03-03 2024-08-31 Resignation 
2 023305   Motinagar 2024-10-01        31 2022-03-03 2024-08-31 Resignation 
3 091134   Amarpur   2024-12-01        31 2025-01-06 NA         &lt;NA&gt;        
# ℹ 1 more variable: net_pay &lt;dbl&gt;


ceiling_date(month, "month") - 1 gives the last day of the month. Employee 023305 of Motinagar resigned on 31 August 2024, but was paid for September and October, ₹66,716 in all. Employee 091134 of Amarpur joined on 6 January 2025, but was paid in full for December 2024. The 28 other joinees of the year were paid from the day they joined, and rightly do not appear.
Each test of this chapter ends by keeping the codes of the flagged employees in an object whose name starts with f_, for flag. We combine them all in Section 7.9.

f_outside &lt;- unique(outside$emp_code)


7.4.2 Pay raised without an order
Basic pay rises with an increment, a promotion or an upgradation, and each needs an order. So every rise in the payroll should have an entry in the change log, with an order number, entered before the salary was paid. Let us first find the rises, month on month. Months paid in part are left out, since their pay is lower for that reason alone.

rises &lt;- payroll |&gt;
  arrange(emp_code, month) |&gt;
  mutate(full = days_paid == days_in_month(month),
         prev_basic = lag(basic_pay), prev_full = lag(full), prev_date = lag(pay_date),
         .by = emp_code) |&gt;
  filter(full, prev_full, basic_pay &gt; prev_basic)
nrow(rises)

[1] 900


lag() gives the value of the row before, here within each employee. There are 900 rises, nearly all of them the increment of July. Now we look for an entry in the change log with an order, entered after the previous month’s salary and on or before this month’s. An inequality join does this.

pay_log &lt;- master_changes |&gt;
  filter(field == "basic_pay", !is.na(order_ref))

backed &lt;- rises |&gt;
  semi_join(pay_log, by = join_by(emp_code, prev_date &lt; entered_on, pay_date &gt;= entered_on))

no_order &lt;- rises |&gt;
  anti_join(backed, by = c("emp_code", "month"))
no_order |&gt;
  select(emp_code, office, month, prev_basic, basic_pay)

# A tibble: 1 × 5
  emp_code office     month      prev_basic basic_pay
  &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;      &lt;date&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
1 089723   Dhaulagarh 2024-11-01      23100     28100


We match on the date of entry, not the date of effect. An increment due in July but entered in October shows in the October payroll. All 18 such late entries are matched. One rise is not. Let us read that employee’s log.

master_changes |&gt;
  filter(emp_code == jump$emp_code) |&gt;
  select(change_type, old_value, new_value, effective_from, order_ref, entered_on, entered_by)

# A tibble: 2 × 7
  change_type old_value new_value effective_from order_ref entered_on entered_by
  &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;date&gt;         &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;date&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;     
1 Increment   22400     23100     2024-07-01     INC/24-2… 2024-07-10 DEO-07    
2 Pay fixati… 23100     28100     2024-11-01     &lt;NA&gt;      2024-11-12 DEO-07    


The basic pay of employee 089723, a clerk of Dhaulagarh, was raised from ₹23,100 to ₹28,100 in November 2024, a rise of 22%. The log calls it a “Pay fixation”, with no order number, entered by user DEO-07. A pay fixation needs an order too. The rise costs about ₹7,650 a month in pay and DA alone.

f_rise &lt;- unique(no_order$emp_code)


7.4.3 More than one increment in a year
In our example, an employee earns one increment a year. Let us count the pay changes of each employee.

pay_changes &lt;- master_changes |&gt;
  filter(field == "basic_pay")

pay_changes |&gt;
  count(emp_code, name = "changes") |&gt;
  count(changes, name = "employees")

# A tibble: 2 × 2
  changes employees
    &lt;int&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1       1       864
